6.text:
The flag of Tonga consists of a red field with a white canton in the upper hoist corner containing a red Greek cross (i.e., a vertical rectangle overlapped with a horizontal rectangle of equal arm length). The matrix in question is therefore an \(m \times n\) array whose entries are drawn exclusively from the two-element set \(\{a,b\}\), with the assignment of \(a\) or \(b\) to each pixel governed by the geometry of the flag.

Any such matrix possesses at most four distinct row vectors, which arise from slicing the flag horizontally:

- rows lying entirely outside the canton produce the constant vector \(a\cdot\mathbf{1}_n\);
- rows lying inside the canton but missing the cross produce a vector whose first \(c\) coordinates equal \(b\) and whose remaining coordinates equal \(a\) (where \(c\) is the canton width);
- rows intersecting only the vertical arm produce a vector whose first \(c\) coordinates contain a single narrow block of \(a\)’s centered inside an otherwise \(b\)-valued interval;
- rows intersecting the horizontal arm produce a vector whose first \(c\) coordinates contain a single wide block of \(a\)’s centered inside an otherwise \(b\)-valued interval.

These four vectors are linearly independent for generic \(a,b\) and sufficiently fine pixel resolution (i.e., when the block boundaries determined by the canton and cross edges are distinct column indices). Consequently the row space has dimension at most 4. Because four independent rows are realized by the geometry above, the bound is attained and the maximal rank is exactly 4.

(The same conclusion follows from the column space: the flag geometry likewise produces at most four distinct columns.)

PUBLIC RESPONSE: 4 (at most four distinct rows, realized and independent for generic a,b)

