5.text:
The Tonga flag geometry produces a matrix \(M=aJ+(b-a)W\) where \(W\) is the 0-1 indicator of the white region (a rectangle minus a centered Greek cross). This partitions the rows of \(W\) into exactly four combinatorial types for any grid aligned to the flag boundaries:

- rows entirely outside the canton (all-zero);
- canton rows missing the cross (one contiguous block of 1s);
- canton rows hitting only the vertical arm (narrower central block of 1s);
- canton rows hitting the horizontal arm (wider central block of 1s).

These four vectors are linearly independent for generic \(a\neq b\), so \(\operatorname{rank}(W)=4\) and therefore \(\operatorname{rank}(M)=4\). No discretization can produce additional row patterns, and the apparent rank-5 upper bound from treating the cross as four separate rectangles is never attained. Claims of rank 2 or 3 arise from under-counting the distinct arm-gap patterns or from over-counting linear dependences once the global additive constant is removed.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: 4 (exactly four distinct row patterns in W, linearly independent for a≠b)

