6.text:
The Tonga flag has a white canton containing a red Greek cross whose vertical bar spans the full canton height. Consequently every canton row intersects that vertical bar. The horizontal bar (full canton width) produces rows whose canton segment is uniformly red—identical to all background rows. This yields exactly two distinct row vectors overall (uniform \(a\); and the fixed \(b\)-left/\(a\)-center/\(b\)-right pattern). Hence \(\operatorname{rank}(M)\le2\), attained whenever \(a\neq b\).

All higher claims rest on erroneously positing additional distinct row patterns that the full-height vertical bar precludes.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {2}

3.text:
The Tonga flag yields a matrix of the form \(M=aJ+cW\) (\(c=b-a\)) whose rows fall into exactly three distinct combinatorial types for any aligned rasterization:

- outside the canton: the constant vector \(a\cdot\mathbf{1}\);
- canton rows intersecting only the vertical arm: \(a\cdot\mathbf{1}+c\cdot g\) where \(g\) is the gapped indicator supported on the white parts of the canton;
- canton rows intersecting the horizontal arm: \(a\cdot\mathbf{1}+c\cdot z\) where \(z\) is the indicator that is zero throughout the canton.

These three explicit vectors remain linearly independent for generic \(a\neq b\). Consequently \(\operatorname{rank}(M)=3\). No finer grid produces a fourth distinct row pattern, and the decomposition via rectangle indicators confirms that \(\operatorname{rank}(W)\le3\), so the bound is tight.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {3}

