8.text:
The Tonga flag consists of a red field with a white canton containing a red Greek cross. The vertical arm of that cross spans the full height of the canton, so every canton row intersects it. Consequently the only two distinct row patterns that can ever arise (for any boundary-aligned rasterization) are:

- uniform-a rows (everything outside the canton, plus every row that intersects the horizontal arm);
- split rows (canton rows missing the horizontal arm): b-left / a-center / b-right inside the canton columns, a elsewhere.

These two vectors are linearly independent whenever a≠b, and the decomposition M=aJ+(b-a)W shows that the white indicator W itself has rank at most 1. No finer or mis-aligned grid can manufacture additional independent patterns, because the vertical arm is continuous and full-height. All higher counts rest on incorrectly treating the horizontal-arm rows as a distinct third (or fourth) pattern; geometrically they coincide with the uniform background.

Hence the maximal attainable rank is exactly 2.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {2}

