2.text:
The Tonga flag has a *centered* red Greek cross inside the white canton; the vertical arm does **not** touch the canton’s top or bottom edges. This produces exactly three distinct row-support patterns for \(W\) (hence for \(M=aJ+(b-a)W\)) under any boundary-aligned rasterization: constant background (all \(a\)), full-canton white rows (above/below the cross), and gapped rows intersecting only the vertical arm. The horizontal-arm rows coincide exactly with the background, so they add no fourth pattern.  

Algebraically the three row types of \(W\) live in a 3-dimensional space (rectangle indicators yield an affine dependence that collapses any apparent fourth direction), so \(\operatorname{rank}(M)\le3\) and the bound is attained on any grid resolving the arms. All rank-2 arguments rest on the incorrect premise that the vertical arm is full-height; once that premise is removed the claimed collapse to two patterns disappears. No discretization yields a fourth independent row.  

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {3}

