The Tonga flag geometry (full-height vertical cross arm inside the canton) forces every canton-height row to intersect that arm, so the horizontal-arm rows are identical to the uniform background. This produces exactly two distinct row vectors in any aligned rasterization. The white-indicator matrix \(W\) is assembled from rectangle indicators whose row/column supports span a space of dimension at most 1, hence \(\operatorname{rank}(M)\le2\) (attained for \(a\neq b\)).  

The rank-4 claims rely on an explicit 4-by-4 minor extracted from columns that are assumed to have independent support patterns, but the shared outer margins of the canton make those columns linearly dependent once the uniform background is accounted for; the minor is in fact singular. The rank-3 position correctly notes the affine collapse of the three centered deviations into a 2-dimensional space, yet still overcounts by treating the horizontal-arm pattern as independent after centering. No discretization yields additional independent directions beyond the two already identified. All higher counts rest on an erroneous geometric premise that the vertical arm fails to reach the canton borders.

